      "side": 1,
      "message": "`resume` reinstates the whole continuation captured during the generator\u0027s first call, and with it that call\u0027s exception handlers, `parameterize` bindings and current ports. `make-for-each-generator` (178) inherits this.\n\n```scheme\n(define g (make-coroutine-generator (lambda (yield) (yield 1) (error \"boom\"))))\n(define a (guard (e (#t \u0027first)) (g)))\n(define b (guard (e (#t \u0027second)) (g)))\n```\n\nThe second call\u0027s error is caught by the first `guard`, and control re-enters the finished `(define a …)` and runs on from there again, every time. If the first call is outside any `guard`, a later `(guard … (g))` doesn\u0027t catch the error at all.\n\nWith a body that yields `(p)`, `(list (parameterize ((p \u0027first)) (g)) (g) (parameterize ((p \u0027third)) (g)))` gives `(first first first)`.\n\nAt least fix the errors: run `(proc yield)` inside a `guard` that marks the generator done and returns the condition, tagged, through `return`. Then have the generator re-raise it in the caller\u0027s own dynamic environment. Parameters and ports need composable continuations (SRFI 226 isn\u0027t exposed yet), so document that limit in the header until then.",
      "revId": "da9acb64fe00b9596e9b9921bb926aaff1a00467",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
